\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We reformulated execution simulation for code repair verification from open-ended output generation to per-test binary classification. Our central finding is that the advantage of this reformulation is complexity-dependent: on function-level code with short, structurally simple outputs, classification and generation achieve near-equivalent accuracy; at repository scale, classification dominates by a wide margin. The generation formulation suffers a compounding failure on complex code---format non-compliance discards the majority of predictions, and the surviving parseable outputs are themselves unreliable, falling below chance-level accuracy (50\%). Classification is immune to this failure mode by construction, as binary verdicts require no structured output parsing. Moreover, at function level, scaling model capacity does not resolve the generation bottleneck: larger \cwm{} models show no accuracy improvement, consistent with a formulation-level rather than capacity-level limitation. Independently, per-test classification provides fine-grained diagnostic signals---identifying which specific tests a patch fails---at equivalent trajectory-level accuracy, with the advantage concentrating in instances that contain multiple fail-to-pass tests.

These results carry practical implications for verification in test-time scaling pipelines. When patches are evaluated against multi-test suites---as in best-of-$N$ sampling or reranking---the choice of verification formulation matters: classification avoids the compounding failure that degrades generation on complex code. At function level, the formulation bottleneck we identify is distinct from model capacity, though whether this extends to repository scale awaits \cwm{} 8B results. Per-test verdicts also unlock downstream applications that trajectory-level verifiers cannot support, including targeted re-repair guided by per-test failure localization rather than binary accept/reject decisions.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
All experiments use a single model family (Qwen3); scaling behavior may differ across architectures. The repo-level same-size comparison rests on a single seed; the second seed's repo-level comparison is cross-size (8B \cls{} vs.\ 4B \cwm{}), not an apple-to-apple same-size match. The 8B--8B comparison awaits \cwm{} 8B training completion. No downstream re-repair experiment validates whether per-test diagnostic signals translate to actual fix improvements. A matched-context ablation (\cls{} evaluated at \texttt{max\_seq\_length}$=$2,048) reduces accuracy by only 0.69\pp{} (83.52$\to$82.83\%), confirming that 97\% of the 25.59\pp{} gap stems from formulation differences, not input context disparity. The oracle analysis assumes ground-truth test labels; real-world per-test advantage is upper-bounded by classifier accuracy.

\paragraph{Future work.}
\cls{}--\cwm{} error complementarity motivates ensemble verification that routes instances by predicted output complexity. Per-test failure signals enable diagnostic re-repair: localizing faults from per-test verdicts rather than discarding failed patches. Multi-model validation and integration with test-time scaling systems (best-of-$N$ with per-test reranking) are natural next steps.

